tant can lodge on your behalf.</p><h3>Step 4 — When to lodge</h3><p>Most clearance certificates issue within days. The ATO asks sellers to allow up to 28, because some applications are checked manually.</p><div class="action-box"><p>The certificate is free, valid for 12 months, and you are under no obligation to use it. So the sensible time to apply is <strong>as soon as you are thinking of selling</strong> — before you list, not once a contract is signed. Applying early takes settlement timing out of the picture entirely.</p></div>{{#if section:validity_12_months}}<h3>The 12-month validity window</h3><p>A certificate is valid for 12 months from the date it is issued, provided your residency status does not change in that period. What the purchaser needs is a certificate that is valid <strong>on the settlement date</strong>.</p>{{/if}}{{#if section:reapplication}}<div class="warning-box"><p><strong>Your contract runs longer than 12 months.</strong> A certificate obtained when the contract was signed can expire before settlement. Time the application against your settlement date rather than the contract date — and if the first one lapses, apply again. A fresh certificate is free.</p></div>{{/if}}<h3>Step 5 — Give it to the purchaser's side</h3><p>Once issued, the certificate goes from you (or your conveyancer or solicitor, acting for you as vendor) to the <strong>purchaser or their representative</strong> — that is who has the withholding obligation and who needs to hold it. Confirm receipt in writing. File 03 has the wording.</p>{{#if has:settlement_date}}<div class="info-box"><p><strong>Your settlement:</strong> {{bind:settlement_date}}. <strong>Lodge by:</strong> {{bind:lodge_by_date}} to allow the full 28 days.</p></div>{{/if}}`;

